{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"Colon cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the colon.","source_span":"Colon cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the colon."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"The colon (large bowel) is the first part of the large intestine and is about 5 feet long.","source_span":"The colon (large bowel) is the first part of the large intestine and is about 5 feet long."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are about 6-8 inches long.","source_span":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are about 6-8 inches long."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"A risk factor for colorectal cancer is having a personal history of chronic ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease for 8 years or more.","source_span":"Having a personal history of chronic ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease for 8 years or more."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"A risk factor for colorectal cancer is having three or more alcoholic drinks per day.","source_span":"Having three or more alcoholic drinks per day."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"Signs of colon cancer include blood in the stool or a change in bowel habits.","source_span":"Signs of colon cancer include blood in the stool or a change in bowel habits."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"High-risk adenomas are colorectal polyps that are 1 centimeter or larger in size or that have cells that look abnormal under a microscope.","source_span":"high-risk adenomas (colorectal polyps that are 1 centimeter or larger in size or that have cells that look abnormal under a microscope)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"The immunochemical FOBT test is also called the fecal immunochemical test, or FIT.","source_span":"This test is also called fecal immunochemical test or FIT."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"A barium enema procedure is also called a lower GI series.","source_span":"This procedure is also called a lower GI series."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","statement":"Virtual colonoscopy is also called colonography or CT colonography.","source_span":"This test is also called colonography or CT colonography."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How long is the colon?","answer":"The colon is the first part of the large intestine and is about 5 feet long."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How long are the rectum and anal canal together?","answer":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are about 6-8 inches long."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is colon cancer?","answer":"Colon cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the colon."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What size or characteristic makes a colorectal adenoma \"high-risk\"?","answer":"A high-risk adenoma is a colorectal polyp that is 1 centimeter or larger in size, or that has cells that look abnormal under a microscope."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is another name for the immunochemical fecal occult blood test?","answer":"It is also called the fecal immunochemical test, or FIT."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is a barium enema procedure also known as?","answer":"A barium enema is also called a lower GI series."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How long must someone have had chronic ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease for it to count as a colon cancer risk factor?","answer":"Having a personal history of chronic ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease for 8 years or more is a risk factor for colon cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"Rectal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the rectum.","source_span":"Rectal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the rectum."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"Signs of rectal cancer include a change in bowel habits or blood in the stool.","source_span":"Signs of rectal cancer include a change in bowel habits or blood in the stool."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"During a digital rectal exam for rectal cancer, the doctor or nurse inserts a lubricated, gloved finger into the lower part of the rectum, and in women the vagina may also be examined.","source_span":"The doctor or nurse inserts a lubricated, gloved finger into the lower part of the rectum to feel for lumps or anything else that seems unusual. In women, the vagina may also be examined."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"The carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) assay is a test that measures the level of CEA in the blood, and CEA is released into the bloodstream from both cancer cells and normal cells.","source_span":"CEA is released into the bloodstream from both cancer cells and normal cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"Higher than normal amounts of CEA in the blood can be a sign of rectal cancer or other conditions.","source_span":"When found in higher than normal amounts, it can be a sign of rectal cancer or other conditions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"Tumor tissue removed during a rectal cancer biopsy may be checked to see if the patient is likely to have the gene mutation that causes HNPCC, which may help plan treatment.","source_span":"Tumor tissue that is removed during the biopsy may be checked to see if the patient is likely to have the gene mutation that causes HNPCC. This may help to plan treatment."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","statement":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are 6-8 inches long.","source_span":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are 6-8 inches long."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is rectal cancer?","answer":"Rectal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the rectum."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the main signs of rectal cancer?","answer":"Signs of rectal cancer include a change in bowel habits or blood in the stool."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What does the CEA assay measure, and what does an elevated result suggest?","answer":"The CEA assay measures the level of carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) in the blood, which is released by both cancer and normal cells; higher than normal amounts can be a sign of rectal cancer or other conditions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How is a digital rectal exam performed on a woman being checked for rectal cancer?","answer":"The doctor or nurse inserts a lubricated, gloved finger into the lower part of the rectum to feel for lumps or abnormalities, and in women the vagina may also be examined."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why might tumor tissue from a rectal cancer biopsy be tested for a gene mutation?","answer":"It may be checked to see if the patient is likely to have the gene mutation that causes HNPCC, which may help plan treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How long are the rectum and anal canal combined?","answer":"Together, the rectum and anal canal make up the last part of the large intestine and are 6-8 inches long."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Colorectal cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in the United States.","source_span":"Colorectal cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in the United States."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"In adults younger than 50 years, the number of new colorectal cancer cases has slowly increased since 1998.","source_span":"in adults younger than 50 years, the number of new colorectal cancer cases has slowly increased since 1998"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"The number of new colorectal cancers and deaths from colorectal cancer are higher in African Americans than in other races.","source_span":"The number of new colorectal cancers and deaths from colorectal cancer are higher in African Americans than in other races."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"The risk of colorectal cancer increases after age 50, and most cases of colorectal cancer are diagnosed after age 50.","source_span":"The risk of colorectal cancer increases after age 50. Most cases of colorectal cancer are diagnosed after age 50."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Having a parent, brother, sister, or child with colorectal cancer doubles a person's risk of colorectal cancer.","source_span":"Having a parent, brother, sister, or child with colorectal cancer doubles a person's risk of colorectal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Drinking 3 or more alcoholic beverages per day increases the risk of colorectal cancer.","source_span":"Drinking 3 or more alcoholic beverages per day increases the risk of colorectal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Taking aspirin lowers the risk of colorectal cancer, and the decrease in risk begins 10 to 20 years after patients start taking aspirin.","source_span":"The decrease in risk begins 10 to 20 years after patients start taking aspirin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Possible harms of daily or every-other-day low-dose aspirin use (100 mg or less) include an increased risk of stroke and bleeding in the stomach and intestines.","source_span":"The possible harms of aspirin use (100 mg or less) daily or every other day include an increased risk of stroke and bleeding in the stomach and intestines."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Combination hormone replacement therapy (estrogen and progestin) lowers the risk of invasive colorectal cancer in postmenopausal women, but colorectal cancer diagnosed while on combination HRT is more likely to be advanced.","source_span":"combination hormone replacement therapy (HRT) that includes both estrogen and progestin lowers the risk of invasive colorectal cancer in postmenopausal women... the cancer is more likely to be advanced when it is diagnosed"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","statement":"Hormone replacement therapy with estrogen only and statins do not affect the risk of colorectal cancer.","source_span":"The following factors do not affect the risk of colorectal cancer:         - Hormone replacement therapy with estrogen only     - Statins"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Where does colorectal cancer rank as a cause of cancer death in the United States?","answer":"Colorectal cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in the United States."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How much does having a first-degree relative with colorectal cancer raise a person's risk?","answer":"Having a parent, brother, sister, or child with colorectal cancer doubles a person's risk of colorectal cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How many alcoholic beverages per day are linked to an increased risk of colorectal cancer?","answer":"Drinking 3 or more alcoholic beverages per day increases the risk of colorectal cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How long after starting aspirin does its protective effect against colorectal cancer begin?","answer":"The decrease in colorectal cancer risk from taking aspirin begins 10 to 20 years after patients start taking it."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are possible harms of daily low-dose aspirin use taken to lower colorectal cancer risk?","answer":"Possible harms include an increased risk of stroke and bleeding in the stomach and intestines."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Does taking statins affect a person's risk of colorectal cancer?","answer":"No, statins do not affect the risk of colorectal cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037_3","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Since what year has the number of new colorectal cancer cases in adults younger than 50 been slowly increasing?","answer":"The number of new colorectal cancer cases in adults younger than 50 has slowly increased since 1998."}
